.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii iscsi-scstd.conf.5
.\"
.TH "ISCSI_SCSTD.CONF" "5" "May 2007" "A. Lehmann, M. Zhang and A. Redlich" "File formats"
.SH "NAME"
/etc/iscsi-scstd.conf \- configuration for iSCSI SCST Target Daemon
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
/etc/iscsi-scstd.conf
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
/etc/iscsi-scstd.conf contains configuration information for the
.B iscsi-scstd (8)
daemon. This is a legacy format for targets, CHAP accounts, iSNS and iSCSI
negotiation parameters. Use scstadmin and /etc/scst.conf for new persistent
configurations.
.P
Directives end at a newline or semicolon. A line whose first non-whitespace
character is `#' is ignored. Inline comments and backslash continuations are
not supported.
.P
Directive and parameter names are case insensitive. The "Yes" and "No"
values for target negotiation parameters are case sensitive.
.P
The file consists of a global part and zero or more "Target" stanzas.
Everything before the first target definition belongs to the global
configuration.

Here is an example:
.nf

IncomingUser joe 12charsecret
OutgoingUser jack 12charsecret2

Target iqn.2007\-05.com.example:storage.disk2.sys1.xyz
    IncomingUser jim 12charsecret3
    OutgoingUser james yetanothersecret
    HeaderDigest None
    DataDigest None
    InitialR2T No
    ImmediateData Yes
    QueuedCommands 32
.fi
.P
Stanzas start with the word "Target" and a globally unique iSCSI target name.
Use a name format defined by the current iSCSI specification. The daemon
creates targets in the order listed.
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
Option names are case insensitive.
.TP
.B [IncomingUser <username> <password>]
Authenticate initiators during discovery. Multiple incoming accounts are
supported. If none is specified, discovery sessions do not require
authentication. The secret must contain at least 12 bytes.
.TP
.B [OutgoingUser <username> <password>]
Authenticate the target to initiators during mutual CHAP discovery. Only one
outgoing account is supported. A later entry replaces the previous one. The
secret must contain at least 12 bytes.
.TP
.B [iSNSServer <address>]
Configure the iSNS server name or address.
.TP
.B [iSNSAccessControl <Yes|No>]
Enable or disable iSNS access control. The default is
.BR No .
.TP
.B Target <name>
Start a target stanza. The target name should be a globally unique name in a
format defined by the current iSCSI specification.
.SH "TARGET OPTIONS"
Option names are case insensitive. The
.B Yes
and
.B No
values are case sensitive.
.TP
.B [IncomingUser <username> <password>]
Authenticate initiators logging in to this target. Multiple incoming accounts
are supported. If none is specified, target login does not require
authentication. The secret must contain at least 12 bytes.
.TP
.B [OutgoingUser <username> <password>]
Authenticate this target to initiators during mutual CHAP login. Only one
outgoing account is supported. A later entry replaces the previous one. The
secret must contain at least 12 bytes.
.TP
.B [Alias <aliasname>]
Accepted for compatibility but ignored by the current legacy parser. Configure
the target alias through scstadmin or the iSCSI sysfs interface.
.TP
.B [HeaderDigest <CRC32C|None|CRC32C,None>]
Offer header digest negotiation. The default is
.BR None .
Header digests are not implemented for discovery sessions.
.TP
.B [DataDigest <CRC32C|None|CRC32C,None>]
Offer data digest negotiation. The default is
.BR None .
Data digests are not implemented for discovery sessions.
.TP
.B [MaxConnections <value>]
The fixed and default value is 1; multiple connections per session are not
supported.
.TP
.B [InitialR2T <Yes|No>]
Control whether an initiator must wait for an R2T before sending SCSI data.
The default is
.BR No .
.TP
.B [ImmediateData <Yes|No>]
Control whether an initiator may include unsolicited data with a command.
The default is
.BR Yes .
.TP
.B [MaxRecvDataSegmentLength <value>]
Set the maximum received data segment length. The minimum is 512 bytes. The
default and upper limit are reported by the registered kernel component.
.TP
.B [MaxXmitDataSegmentLength <value>]
Set the maximum transmitted data segment length. The minimum is 512 bytes.
The default and upper limit are reported by the registered kernel component;
the negotiated value is also limited by the initiator.
.TP
.B [MaxBurstLength <value>]
Set the maximum data burst length. The minimum is 512 bytes. The default and
upper limit are reported by the registered kernel component.
.TP
.B [FirstBurstLength <value>]
Set the maximum unsolicited first burst length. The minimum is 512 bytes. The
default is 65536 bytes, limited by the maximum reported by the registered
kernel component.
.TP
.B [DefaultTime2Wait <value>]
The only configurable value is 0.
.TP
.B [DefaultTime2Retain <value>]
The only configurable value is 0.
.TP
.B [MaxOutstandingR2T <value>]
Set the maximum number of outstanding R2T requests. The default is 32 and the
valid range is 1 through 65535.
.TP
.B [DataPDUInOrder <Yes|No>]
Control data PDU ordering. The local default is
.BR No .
.TP
.B [DataSequenceInOrder <Yes|No>]
Control data sequence ordering. The local default is
.BR No .
.TP
.B [ErrorRecoveryLevel <value>]
The fixed and default value is 0.
.TP
.B [QueuedCommands <value>]
Set the command window for each session. The default is 32 and the configured
range is 1 through 2048, further limited by the registered kernel component.
.TP
.B [RspTimeout <value>]
Set the command response timeout in seconds. The default is 90 and the valid
range is 2 through 65535.
.TP
.B [NopInInterval <value>]
Set the idle interval in seconds between NOP-In requests. The default is 30,
the valid range is 0 through 65535, and 0 disables NOP-In requests.
.TP
.B [NopInTimeout <value>]
Set the NOP-In response timeout in seconds. The default is 30 and the valid
range is 2 through 65535. It should not exceed
.BR RspTimeout .
.TP
.B [MaxSessions <value>]
Set the maximum concurrent sessions for the target. The default value 0 means
no limit; the valid range is 0 through 65535.
.SH "LIMITATIONS"
Header and data digests are not implemented for discovery sessions.
.SH "SECURITY"
This file can contain CHAP secrets and should have restricted ownership and
permissions. Without an applicable
.B IncomingUser
entry, discovery or target login does not require authentication.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR iscsi-scstd (8),
.BR "RFC 7143"
